Source: Wikipedia — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinendra_Kumar_Roy$b$, 'en', $b$https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dinendrakumar-ray.jpg?width=640$b$, $b$Writer, Translator, Editor, Children's writer, Novelist, Author$b$, $b$Meherpur District$b$, NULL, 'day', 'day');
INSERT INTO people (id, name, birth_date, death_date, bio, lang, photo_url, occupation, birth_place, death_place, birth_precision, death_precision) VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), $b$Dipendranath Bandyopadhyay$b$, DATE '1936-11-10', DATE '1979-01-14', $b$Dipendranath Bandyopadhyay (1936–1979) was a Bengali writer, editor, correspondent and political activist. He reshaped Bengali prose writing in late 1950 to 1970, with his deft portrayal of love, protest and anger in post-independence Bengali milieu. Associated with left political activities as student, active member of the Bengal Provincial Student Federation (BPSF), the provincial branch of All India Students Federation (AISF) Dipendratnath was a narrator of the turbulent socio-political vortex of his time. He wrote 51 short stories, 5 novels - including one unfinished - and significant number of reportage or newspaper articles, during his short-lived creative period. Specially his reportages were popular in Bengali readers. Increased political and organisational activity reduced his literary activity which decreased to a trickle in his twilight years.

Bandyopadhyay was posthumously awarded a Friends of Liberation War Honour, the third-highest state award given by the government of Bangladesh for foreigners or non-nationals.

Education: University of Calcutta, Scottish Church College

Source: Wikipedia — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dipendranath_Bandyopadhyay$b$, 'en', NULL, $b$Writer$b$, NULL, $b$Kolkata$b$, 'day', 'day');
INSERT INTO people (id, name, birth_date, death_date, bio, lang, photo_url, occupation, birth_place, death_place, birth_precision, death_precision) VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), $b$Dom Antonio$b$, DATE '1643-01-01', DATE '1695-01-01', $b$Dom Antonio de Rozario (c. 1643 – 1695) was a Christian missionary from Bengal. He was the first writer of Bangla prose.

